  "content": {
    "id": "p17-q8",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p17-q8-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Using the Factor Theorem, factorise $P(x)=x^3+3x^2-46x+72$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(x)=(x-2)(x-4)(x+9)$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}P(2)&=2^3+3(2)^2-46(2)+72\\\\P(2)&=0\\end{align*}$$\nBy the Factor Theorem, $x-2$ is a factor. Polynomial division gives\n$$\\begin{align*}P(x)&=(x-2)(x^2+5x-36)\\\\P(x)&=(x-2)(x-4)(x+9).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q8-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, determine the number of real roots of the polynomial $Q(x)=P(x^2)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p17-q8-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Four real roots: ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=\\pm\\sqrt{2},\\ \\pm2",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p17-q8-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Substitute ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x^2",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " into the factorisation; ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x^2+9",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " has no real zeroes.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}Q(x)&=P(x^2)\\\\Q(x)&=(x^2-2)(x^2-4)(x^2+9).\\end{align*}$$\nBy the null factor law, $Q(x)=0$ when $x^2=2$, $x^2=4$ or $x^2=-9$. The first two give $x=\\pm\\sqrt{2}$ and $x=\\pm2$; the last has no real solutions. Therefore, $Q(x)$ has exactly four real roots.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      }
    ]
  },
